00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.320
Buy one lesson and get one free.
Check out the link in the description box.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.280
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:15.359 --> 00:00:19.839
Today's video is the second installment
in the series "Mystery Languages".

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.040
If you haven't seen the first video 
in the  "Mystery Languages" series,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.200
you can check it out right here.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:26.980
But basically, this is how it works:

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.480
I will play you an audio sample of a language 
and then I'll show you a visual sample

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.660
of the language in it's written form 
and you can guess what language it is.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.240
But whether you guess correctly or not,
that doesn't matter.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.900
The important thing is that you leave your guesses 
and the reasons for your guesses 
in the comments down below

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.980
And that will spark some interesting discussion.

00:00:45.179 --> 00:00:47.319
So, let's get right into the first language.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.280
Please, listen :

00:01:07.359 --> 00:01:10.659
Now let's have a look at a few written samples 
of that same language:

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.000
So what language do you think it is?

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:26.219
The language is Cebuano.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.180
A language spoken on the island of Cebu
 in the Philippines

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:34.000
If you guessed Tagalog or Filipino which is 
the official language which is based on Tagalog,

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.440
then that's a pretty good guess because Tagalog 
and Cebuano are closely related languages.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.940
I've studied Tagalog and I'm fairly familiar with it.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:47.920
And when I look at Cebuano, sometimes I don't know 
if I'm looking at Tagalog with lots of vocabulary 
I don't know or whether I'm looking at Cebuano.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.500
Cebuano is sometimes referred to as "Bisaya",

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:55.640
which is actually a group of closely related languages
that are spoken in the Visayas region in the Philippines.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.560
But some people like to refer to all the Bisaya languages 
as a single language with different dialects.

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:04.659
That's because all the Bisaya languages 
are quite closely related

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:07.759
but also because, well, there's power in numbers.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.060
You may have noticed the Spanish vocabulary 
in the sample sentences.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.900
That's because Cebuano has lots of Spanish loanwords, 
just like Tagalop does.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.960
In fact, Cebuano has even more Spanish 
loanwords than Tagalog.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.020
Ok, on to the second language.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:20.879
Please, listen:

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.300
And again, let's have a look at some written samples.

00:02:52.659 --> 00:02:53.899
So, what language is it?

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.120
It's the Lao language.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.800
Lao is a language spoken 
in the Southeast Asian nation of Laos.

00:03:02.939 --> 00:03:05.299
It's a member of the Tai-Kadai language family,

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.280
which is spoken in some parts of Southeast Asia,
southern China in northern India.

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:12.239
If you thought the language was Thai,
then you were very close

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.160
because Thai and Lao are very closely related
and to a large degree, they are mutually intelligible.

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:18.719
They are both tonal languages,

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:23.160
but one notable difference is that 
Thai has five tones while Lao has six stones

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:27.759
They are also written in a slightly different script
but the scripts themselves are also closely related.

00:03:27.759 --> 00:03:30.559
And if you can read one, 
then reading the other shouldn't be too hard.

00:03:30.699 --> 00:03:35.319
Some of you may have noticed 
the tones in the language sample, 
which make the language sound kind of melodic.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.840
And you might have guessed one of the chinese 
languages like Mandarin or Cantonese.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.060
And those guesses would make sense, because
those are tonal languages just like Lao and Thai are.

00:03:43.219 --> 00:03:45.219
Ok, on to the third language.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:45.960
Please, listen:

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.620
And again, let's see a bit of the language, visually :

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.460
So what language is it?

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.939
It's the Romanian language

00:04:26.939 --> 00:04:33.099
If you speak any Romance languages, then 
you probably noticed right away that there was 
a lot of romance vocabulary in the samples.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.240
But the pronunciation might have seemed 
kind of unusual to you.

00:04:36.339 --> 00:04:40.259
That's because Romanian is a Romance language 
with some Slavic influence.

00:04:40.399 --> 00:04:44.199
That influence affects the pronunciation 
and also the vocabulary of the language.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.220
You may have also noticed the little glyphs 
above the letter "a" and below the letter "t".

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.000
Those glyphs are actually an integral part of those letters.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.480
And those letters along with this, this and this

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:57.740
give you a quick hint that you're looking at Romanian 
and not at a different Romance language.

00:04:57.899 --> 00:05:00.379
Romanian is spoken in Romania and Moldova

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:03.880
and is geographically isolated 
from the other major Romance languages.

00:05:04.019 --> 00:05:07.939
The Slavic influence on the language makes it distinct 
from the other Romance languages.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:12.720
But Romanian is also said to be the closest 
Romance language to the original Latin.

00:05:12.959 --> 00:05:14.459
In grammar, that's probably true.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:19.520
But in some other areas like phonology, it may not be 
as close to the original Latin as say Italian.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.340
Well, I hope you enjoyed guessing
today's Mystery Languages.

00:05:22.399 --> 00:05:24.939
Leave your guesses and your reasons
in the comments down below.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.680
And also let me know if you liked this series 
and you think I should continue doing it.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:32.900
And again, I want to say thank you to all of my 
Patreon supporters for your continued support,

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:37.140
especially these ones, right here on the screen, 
